# Project status

Rocci is an active experiment with a working compiler core, examples, editor
support, local runtime, macOS development bundle, and an emerging documentation
generator. It is not a stable production framework yet.

## Working today

- `.rocci` parsing and deterministic lowering to Roc.
- Components, fixtures, structural directives, scoped CSS, and Datastar actions.
- Standalone `@context`, `@init`, and `@on` applications.
- `.rocdown` Markdown, page metadata, Roc and Rocci regions, links, themes, and preview.
- Build, inspect, AST, run, view, and browse commands.
- VS Code and Zed language registration plus LSP diagnostics and navigation.
- Tao/Wry embedded preview windows.
- Local ad-hoc-signed macOS application bundles.
- Rocs nested content, curated navigation, static HTML, sitemap, robots, and `llms.txt`.

## Deliberate current limitations

- Rocs dynamic island splicing is not implemented.
- Rocs search, clean per-page Markdown, aliases, and development watch mode are pending.
- The desktop host manages one main window.
- Windows and Linux installers are not implemented.
- Production signing, notarization, updates, tray behavior, and deep links are absent.
- Native capabilities such as dialogs, filesystem access, and notifications are planned.
- Cross-platform CI coverage is incomplete.

## Stable architectural direction

The project intends to keep these boundaries:

1. Rocci and Rocdown are explicit source syntaxes that lower to ordinary Roc or semantic content data.
2. HTML and local HTTP remain the interface between backend and browser or webview.
3. Datastar is pinned per application and never auto-updated by `run`.
4. Rocs keeps its catalog and static prose processing in Rust while authoring site chrome in Rocci.
5. Dynamic behavior remains opt-in and identifiable in source.

## Before adopting

Read the [architecture](/concepts/architecture/), run the
[examples](/examples/), and review the repository roadmap. Pin the exact Rocci,
Roc, and Datastar revisions used by your application.
